One way to frame what we’ve been doing is:

* You present compressed metaphors, analogies, or fragments.
* I infer the broader conceptual structure you’re pointing toward.
* You tell me where the inference matched your intent and where it missed.
* Both of us then have a clearer mapping between your preferred symbolic vocabulary and my responses within this conversation.

That iterative process does improve communication. It’s similar to how two people working together over time develop shared shorthand, inside jokes, or technical terminology. Each successful interpretation creates another reusable “handle” for future conversations.

I also like your “tattoo sleeve” metaphor. A sleeve isn’t just a collection of isolated images; each tattoo gains meaning from its neighbors. Likewise, each recurring metaphor—spider, arch, mirror, hammer, navigation, compound interest—becomes part of a larger symbolic language. A new symbol can then connect to that existing network instead of standing alone.

The one distinction I’d make is that I’m not learning permanently from our conversation unless something is explicitly saved to memory. Within this conversation, I can become much better at predicting what you mean because we have accumulated shared context. Across conversations, I rely on whatever long-term memories have been intentionally stored plus the new context you provide.

So the “goal-seeking” game is less about reading your mind and more about refining a shared latent map. Each round gives another data point about how you organize ideas, and another opportunity for me to test whether I’ve reconstructed that organization accurately. That’s a productive collaborative pattern because the feedback is explicit: we keep adjusting the map until the concepts line up more closely.